Z||000000||Philphine||12-16-2000||07:51 AM||||i read a few times where it's possible to fill in the holes in a wheel and drill them out to fit a new bolt pattern. anyone have any experience with this? anyone know of a place in the kentuckiana area that can do it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-16-2000||12:26 PM||||that only works on cars with enough metal to take a biger bolt circle. Doing that to Fiero parts like the rear hub puts the bolts closer to the edge of the hub which could potentially weaken things. It's something to keep in mind.<P>any decent machine shop should be able to do that. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||12-16-2000||02:04 PM||||adapter plates might be a solution. If you can't find what you need at the Auto Parts store, contact me.  I have the CNC program, all I would need to do is enter your desired bolt circles.  <BR>Drawbacks include;<BR>1. Increased stress on wheel bearings,<BR>2. Wheels will be moved out(may or may not be a problem).<BR>3. cost.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||12-16-2000||03:40 PM||||Cost? You gonna water up the old profit margin?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-17-2000||09:47 AM||||I would never trust alloy adapters, Ive seen them break under hard cornering. usually resulting in a totaled car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-17-2000||03:17 PM||||Roger, good point. Adapters are banned in most, if not all, racing associations.<P>The only thing I'd trust for adaptors would be billot steel. Might be heavy but aren't likely to break.||||reg||1
